{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully. The user wants me to implement a `solve(case)` function for grammar-guided node reduction.\n\nLet me understand the requirements:\n1. Reduce a parse tree by removing nodes\n2. Consider deeper nodes before shallower ones (process deeper nodes first)\n3. Never remove: root, required symbol nodes, nodes with retained children\n4. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented after removal\n5. Use node ID as stable tie-break at equal depth\n6. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order\n\nLet me think through the algorithm:\n\n1. Parse the nodes from the case\n2. Build the tree structure (parent-child relationships)\n3. Process nodes in order: deeper nodes first, with node ID as tie-break at equal depth\n4. For each node (in that order):\n   - Skip if not removable\n   - Skip if it's a required symbol\n   - Skip if it has retained children\n   - Tentatively remove and check if all required symbols are still represented\n   - If yes, remove it (add to removal order)\n   - If no, keep it\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nSo the conditions for removal:\n- `removable` must be True\n- The node's symbol must NOT be a required symbol\n- The node must NOT have any retained children at the time of removal\n- After removal, all required symbols must still be represented\n\nThe traversal order: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" - process deepest nodes first. At equal depth, use node ID as tie-break.\n\nNode IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. For tie-breaking at equal depth, we sort by node ID. The problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" - lexicographic ordering of node IDs seems appropriate.\n\nLet me trace through example fg1-se-pe-01-b-000:\n\nNodes:\n- root: depth=0, symbol=root, removable=false\n- n1: depth=1, symbol=s1, removable=false\n- n2: depth=1, symbol=s2, removable=true\n- n3: depth=1, symbol=s0, removable=false\n- n4: depth=2, symbol=s1, removable=true, parent=n2\n- n5: depth=2, symbol=s2, removable=false, parent=n3\n- n6: depth=2, symbol=s0, removable=true, parent=n1\n\nRequired symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nProcessing order (deepest first, then by node ID):\nDepth 2: n4, n5, n6 (sorted by ID)\nDepth 1: n1, n2, n3 (sorted by ID)\nDepth 0: root\n\nInitially retained: {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6}\n\nProcess n4 (depth=2, symbol=s1, removable=true, parent=n2):\n- Check: s1 is a required symbol? Yes! So we can't remove it.\n- Skip.\n\nProcess n5 (depth=2, symbol=s2, removable=false):\n- removable is false, skip.\n\nProcess n6 (depth=2, symbol=s0, removable=true, parent=n1):\n- symbol s0 not in required symbols ✓\n- no children (it's a leaf) ✓\n- After removal: symbols represented = {root(root), s1(n1), s2(n2), s0(n3), s1(n4), s2(n5)} \n  - s1 is still there (from n1, n4), root is still there\n  - All required symbols still represented ✓\n- Remove n6. Retained: {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5}\n- removal_order: [n6]\n\nProcess n1 (depth=1, symbol=s1, removable=false):\n- removable is false, skip.\n\nProcess n2 (depth=1, symbol=s2, removable=true):\n- symbol s2 not in required symbols ✓\n- Check retained children of n2: n4 is still retained and is a child of n2\n- n4 is retained, so n2 has retained children → cannot remove\n- Skip.\n\nProcess n3 (depth=1, symbol=s0, removable=false):\n- removable is false, skip.\n\nProcess root (depth=0, removable=false):\n- Skip.\n\nFinal retained (sorted): [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root]\nRemoval order: [n6]\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider the required symbol check. The problem says \"Never remove... a required symbol\". Does this mean a node whose symbol is in required_symbols? Or a node that is somehow registered as required?\n\nLooking at the case: required_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]. So nodes with symbol \"root\" or \"s1\" cannot be removed.\n\nn4 has symbol \"s1\", so it cannot be removed (even though it's marked removable=true).\n\nThat seems right.\n\nNow let me think about \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\":\n- After removing the node, check if every symbol in required_symbols is still represented by at least one retained node.\n\nLet me verify my trace:\nAfter removing n6, retained nodes have symbols: root(root), s1(n1), s2(n2), s0(n3), s1(n4), s2(n5)\n- root: represented ✓\n- s1: represented (n1, n4) ✓\n\nSo removal of n6 is valid.\n\nCan n2 be removed? n2 has retained child n4. So no.\nIf n4 were removed first... but n4 can't be removed because its symbol is s1 (required).\n\nSo the final answer for case 0:\n- retained: [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root] (sorted)\n- removed_order: [n6]\n\nWait, should I sort IDs lexicographically? Let me check: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root. Lexicographic: n1 \u003c n2 \u003c n3 \u003c n4 \u003c n5 \u003c root. That seems fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the sort. \"Return sorted retained IDs\" - sorted how? Alphabetically/lexicographically seems most natural for strings.\n\nLet me now think about the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case.get(\"payload\", case)\n    \n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Validate required fields\n    for node in nodes:\n        for field in [\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"]:\n            if field not in node:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Node missing field: {field}\")\n    \n    # Build structures\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            if node[\"parent\"] not in node_map:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {node['parent']} not found\")\n            children[node[\"parent\"]].append(node[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Sort nodes: deepest first, then by node ID (lexicographic) for tie-breaking\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    # Track retained nodes\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removal_order = []\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Skip if not removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if symbol is required\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if has retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(c in retained for c in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentatively remove: check if all required symbols still represented\n        retained_after = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_after = {node_map[rid][\"symbol\"] for rid in retained_after}\n        \n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\n            retained = retained_after\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained_ids\": sorted_retained,\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nWait, I need to be careful about the case structure. Looking at the input, `case` is one of the objects from `cases`, so it has `case_id` and `payload`. Let me check...\n\nThe problem says \"Implement `solve(case)`\" - I'll assume case is the full case object with `payload` key.\n\nLet me also think about edge cases:\n- What if the case itself doesn't have a \"payload\"? Raise ValueError.\n- What if \"nodes\" or \"required_symbols\" are missing? Raise ValueError.\n\nLet me also verify: the \"root\" node is in required_symbols for the test cases. And root has removable=false, so it would be skipped anyway. But if someone had removable=true on a required-symbol node, the required_symbol check would catch it.\n\nLet me also reconsider the output format. The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It says \"JSON-serializable\" and \"exact named fields.\" I'll use `retained_ids` and `removal_order`.\n\nLet me trace through case b-001 to double-check:\n\nNodes:\n- root: depth=0, symbol=root, removable=false\n- n1: depth=1, symbol=s1, removable=false\n- n2: depth=1, symbol=s2, removable=true\n- n3: depth=1, symbol=s0, removable=false\n- n4: depth=2, symbol=s1, removable=true, parent=n2\n- n5: depth=2, symbol=s2, removable=false, parent=n3\n- n6: depth=2, symbol=s0, removable=true, parent=n1\n\nRequired: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nProcessing order (depth desc, then ID asc):\n- n4 (depth=2): symbol=s1 (required) → skip\n- n5 (depth=2): removable=false → skip\n- n6 (depth=2): symbol=s0, removable=true, no children\n  - After removal: retained symbols = {root, s1(n1), s2(n2), s0(n3), s1(n4), s2(n5)}\n  - required_symbols ⊆ symbols_after ✓ → remove n6\n  - removal_order = [n6], retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5}\n- n1 (depth=1): removable=false → skip\n- n2 (depth=1): symbol=s2, removable=true, children=[n4], n4 in retained → has retained child → skip\n- n3 (depth=1): removable=false → skip\n- root (depth=0): removable=false → skip\n\nResult: retained=[n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root], removal_order=[n6]\n\nSame result as case b-000. That makes sense since the structure is identical, just utilities differ (which don't affect the reduction logic here).\n\nCase b-002 would also be the same.\n\nThe algorithm looks correct. Let me finalize the implementation.\n\nOne more thing: the problem says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" I should validate that:\n- nodes and required_symbols fields exist\n- Each node has required fields\n- Parent references are valid (exist in node_map)\n- There's exactly one root (node with parent=null)\n\nLet me write the final implementation and format it as JSON.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate top-level structure\\n    if \\\"payload\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing 'payload' in case\\\")\\n    payload = case[\\\"payload\\\"]\\n\\n    for required_key in (\\\"nodes\\\", \\\"required_symbols\\\"):\\n        if required_key not in payload:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing '{required_key}' in payload\\\")\\n\\n    nodes = payload[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or len(nodes) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"'nodes' must be a non-empty list\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"], list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"'required_symbols' must be a list\\\")\\n\\n    # Validate each node and build lookup\\n    node_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        for field in (\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"):\\n            if field not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Node missing required field '{field}': {node}\\\")\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate node id: {nid}\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n\\n    # Validate parent references and build children map\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    roots_found = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if parent is None:\\n            roots_found.append(node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n        else:\\n            if parent not in node_map:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Node '{node['id']}' references unknown parent '{parent}'\\\")\\n            children[parent].append(node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    if len(roots_found) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Expected exactly one root node (parent=null), found {len(roots_found)}\\\")\\n\\n    # Sort nodes: deepest first; at equal depth, sort by node ID (lexicographic) as stable tie-break\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n\\n    # Track retained set and removal order\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removal_order = []\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: must be flagged removable\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: never remove a node whose symbol is required\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: never remove a node that still has retained children\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Interestingness oracle: all required symbols must still be represented\\n        retained_after = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_after = {node_map[rid][\\\"symbol\\\"] for rid in retained_after}\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Commit the removal\\n        retained = retained_after\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_ids\\\": sorted(retained),\\n        \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01ThrHpz6yEY6zGhUTK3rbP7","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":2191,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":56,"output_tokens":3669,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2836},"service_tier":"standard"}}